-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Cauldron
  Publisher   Palace Software, 1985
  Version     1.1

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch:

  File        Cauldron (1985)(Palace).z80
  MD5         d0788bcf0f43328508bba9d1a1d7370d

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures become Next hardware sprites, so they keep their own colours
  and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is unchanged.

  Pace        original (-0.45% measured in JNext 1.1.104 headless, the plain
              .nex as control, input keyed to the main loop 0x809D, lockstep
              checked; round 2: the plot hook paced by the hook library's
              limiter (hlib_pace: the unpatched plot's JNext time by width,
              shift and attribute type, x 0.95), the attribute hooks' limiter
              kept; 3 of 30 trials warn that interrupts were held off longer
              than the original (59.9 ms against 39.9), as before round 2)

  Changes     1.0: Dean Woodyatt's build 43c1ca49
              1.1: figures painted in their own colour keep it over scenery
              (the bats over trees); trainer; loading screen; whole alt ROM
              image and the start-up checks; the hooks sped up (an image
              cache), the caller's speed kept; no one-frame figure vanish
              (sprite switches deferred and timed to the beam); pace held to
              the original's with a limiter on the plot hook

  Note        Dean Woodyatt's Cauldron engine with one change: a bat (and
              every figure the game paints in its own colour) keeps that
              colour over the trees and the cavern rocks, where it used to
              take theirs. The witch, the bats and the other figures are
              hardware sprites with a black outline; the scenery keeps its
              colours. A figure's old sprite stays until its new one replaces
              it, in one switch timed to the beam, so no figure vanishes for a
              frame. Loading screen from the tape.

